1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,672
The Journey of a Paper

2
00:00:04,222 --> 00:00:07,790
On March 25, Pakistan delivered a fifteen-point U.S.

3
00:00:07,830 --> 00:00:11,744
ceasefire plan to Iran. According to Pakistani officials,

4
00:00:11,784 --> 00:00:18,960
it offered sanctions relief in exchange for rolling back Iran's nuclear program, limits on its missiles,

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,874
and the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz.

6
00:00:21,914 --> 00:00:29,516
The White House declined to discuss the plan, or to say whom in Iran it was negotiating with.

7
00:00:29,866 --> 00:00:37,148
That afternoon, Iranian state media, citing an unnamed senior official, said Iran rejected the American proposal.

8
00:00:37,188 --> 00:00:43,110
It laid out five conditions of its own: an end to attacks and to the killing of its leaders,

9
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:49,266
guarantees that the war would not happen again, reparations, an end to fighting on every front,

10
00:00:49,306 --> 00:00:53,696
and Iranian sovereignty over the Strait of Hormuz.

11
00:00:54,046 --> 00:01:01,711
Earlier that day, an Iranian drone hit the fuel depot at Kuwait International Airport, starting a large fire.

12
00:01:01,751 --> 00:01:04,680
Kuwait said it shot down six other drones.

13
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:09,345
The fire burned for three days and was not put out until March 28.

14
00:01:09,385 --> 00:01:18,452
Debris from an intercepted missile fell near Amman, and another missile landed in a field near Hadera in Israel.

15
00:01:18,802 --> 00:01:23,359
In Tehran, two cruise missile production sites were destroyed.

16
00:01:23,399 --> 00:01:27,363
In northwestern Iran, airstrikes killed eleven people.

17
00:01:27,403 --> 00:01:34,704
Iran claimed it fired cruise missiles at the carrier USS Abraham Lincoln; the ship was not damaged.

18
00:01:34,744 --> 00:01:39,375
In western Anbar, a U.S. airstrike killed seven Iraqi soldiers.

19
00:01:39,415 --> 00:01:43,976
The papers were moving, and so were the bombs.

20
00:01:44,326 --> 00:01:47,207
One side said: give up what you have.

21
00:01:47,247 --> 00:01:50,603
The other said: pay for what you have done.

22
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:54,472
Fifteen American points, five Iranian conditions.

23
00:01:54,512 --> 00:01:57,472
Only one word appeared on both papers.

24
00:01:57,512 --> 00:02:02,052
Hormuz. And the two countries meant opposite things by it.

25
00:02:02,092 --> 00:02:06,716
One paper went out, and one paper came back.

26
00:02:07,066 --> 00:02:09,630
A sheaf of paper. Fifteen points.

27
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:16,337
Tariq Mehmood, Pakistan's assistant secretary for West Asia, counted the pages first.

28
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:18,547
Seven pages of hard English.

29
00:02:18,587 --> 00:02:24,071
Three weeks earlier he had memorized a number that belonged to no one.

30
00:02:24,111 --> 00:02:28,490
Seventy-two. It evaporated before it reached Washington.

31
00:02:28,530 --> 00:02:30,541
This time there was paper.

32
00:02:30,581 --> 00:02:33,704
Paper does not evaporate.

33
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:37,520
Outside, the Margalla Hills sat in morning haze.

34
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:42,487
Islamabad was a city planned too well, a city that did not suit war.

35
00:02:42,527 --> 00:02:49,426
In the summer of 1971, Kissinger faked a stomach ache near here, vanished, and flew to Beijing.

36
00:02:49,466 --> 00:02:53,224
Even then, this city carried other people's secrets.

37
00:02:53,264 --> 00:02:58,724
Now it stood at the center of the world's most tangled war.

38
00:02:59,074 --> 00:03:02,580
The document was wrapped in diplomatic language.

39
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:05,240
Unwrapped, its sentences were short.

40
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,899
Put it down, and we will let you go.

41
00:03:07,939 --> 00:03:10,485
Tehran would not accept this paper.

42
00:03:10,525 --> 00:03:12,553
But a paper would come back.

43
00:03:12,593 --> 00:03:16,542
Measuring the distance between two papers was his job.

44
00:03:16,582 --> 00:03:18,463
The handover ended at ten.

45
00:03:18,503 --> 00:03:23,864
In his notebook he wrote only the time, not the contents.

46
00:03:24,214 --> 00:03:28,055
Leila Shirazi had written her resignation three times.

47
00:03:28,095 --> 00:03:30,713
The first, the day South Pars burned.

48
00:03:30,753 --> 00:03:33,732
The second, the dawn after Natanz was hit.

49
00:03:33,772 --> 00:03:37,109
The third, now, just before two in the morning.

50
00:03:37,149 --> 00:03:40,199
Each time she wrote it and did not save it.

51
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:45,517
The cursor hovered over the button, she paused, and she closed the window.

52
00:03:45,557 --> 00:03:50,228
Tomorrow, then. Tomorrow was always another dawn.

53
00:03:50,578 --> 00:03:53,668
The fifteen points had crossed her desk too.

54
00:03:53,708 --> 00:04:00,639
The technical annex on the nuclear clauses: centrifuge counts, enrichment caps, inspection cycles.

55
00:04:00,679 --> 00:04:02,773
She had written those numbers.

56
00:04:02,813 --> 00:04:10,313
Sentences from her own hand were now traveling through Islamabad to Tehran, the city where her aunt lived.

57
00:04:10,353 --> 00:04:13,301
The document went in the name of a nation.

58
00:04:13,341 --> 00:04:17,336
The numbers came from one person's hand.

59
00:04:17,686 --> 00:04:22,205
The list of sites for inspection: Natanz, Isfahan, Arak.

60
00:04:22,245 --> 00:04:26,520
And Fordow. The draft from above had left Fordow out.

61
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:30,102
The reason was one line: already inoperable.

62
00:04:30,142 --> 00:04:32,545
She typed the word in herself.

63
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:40,361
Then she wrote her cable. If a counterproposal comes with the rejection, the door is not closed.

64
00:04:40,401 --> 00:04:44,156
Do not kill the counterproposal.

65
00:04:44,506 --> 00:04:47,003
Fire was more frightening by day.

66
00:04:47,043 --> 00:04:50,463
At night, fire is light. By day, it is smoke.

67
00:04:50,503 --> 00:04:55,307
Fatima al-Rashid, a pharmacist, saw it from behind her counter.

68
00:04:55,347 --> 00:04:59,767
A black column rose in the southern sky, over the airport.

69
00:04:59,807 --> 00:05:03,765
Her customers turned toward the glass door together.

70
00:05:03,805 --> 00:05:10,952
No one screamed. Twenty-six days of war had taken the screaming out of people.

71
00:05:11,302 --> 00:05:17,334
Burning jet fuel made a different smoke, and as a pharmacist she knew what was in it.

72
00:05:17,374 --> 00:05:24,835
In 1991, when a retreating army set the oil wells on fire, this country had lived once under a black sky.

73
00:05:24,875 --> 00:05:29,907
She took boxes of masks from the shelf and stacked them on the counter.

74
00:05:29,947 --> 00:05:31,907
She did not charge for them.

75
00:05:31,947 --> 00:05:34,550
Three people already wanted inhalers.

76
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:36,812
She had eleven.

77
00:05:37,162 --> 00:05:41,970
The radio said Pakistan had carried the American paper to Tehran.

78
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:45,922
One way, a paper crossed. The other way, drones came.

79
00:05:45,962 --> 00:05:49,801
Her five-year-old peeked out from under the counter.

80
00:05:49,841 --> 00:05:51,889
"Mom, is that a cloud?" "No.

81
00:05:51,929 --> 00:05:55,842
Clouds are white." Talks and bombs do not take turns.

82
00:05:55,882 --> 00:06:01,208
They come together, on the same day, under the same sky.

83
00:06:01,558 --> 00:06:05,242
Captain Kim Junho laid the two papers side by side.

84
00:06:05,282 --> 00:06:07,944
One, a summary of the fifteen points.

85
00:06:07,984 --> 00:06:12,983
The other, the five conditions just read on Iranian state television.

86
00:06:13,023 --> 00:06:15,320
Fifteen was what America wanted.

87
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,365
Five was what Iran demanded.

88
00:06:17,405 --> 00:06:23,280
The distance between them was why the ships he knew by name were still at anchor.

89
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:28,340
At Panmunjom, closing such a distance took two years.

90
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:31,775
Sarah Callahan came in with her notebook.

91
00:06:31,815 --> 00:06:34,596
"Iran said no. So is it over, Major?"

92
00:06:34,636 --> 00:06:37,340
Major Henderson set down his coffee.

93
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:41,609
"Ever seen a negotiation where the first answer was yes?

94
00:06:41,649 --> 00:06:46,336
The first answer has to be no, so the next sentence can start.

95
00:06:46,376 --> 00:06:49,232
Rejection is part of the negotiation."

96
00:06:49,272 --> 00:06:52,282
Sarah wrote it down, then looked at Kim.

97
00:06:52,322 --> 00:06:54,920
"And you, Captain?"

98
00:06:55,270 --> 00:07:00,111
Kim pointed to the last line: sovereignty over the Strait of Hormuz.

99
00:07:00,151 --> 00:07:03,197
"This is not a rejection. It's a price tag.

100
00:07:03,237 --> 00:07:06,930
Iran has already been choosing which ships may pass.

101
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:11,452
For the ships I count, it doesn't matter who holds sovereignty.

102
00:07:11,492 --> 00:07:13,390
The door just has to open."

103
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:19,604
He smiled. It was the first time in this war Sarah had seen him smile.

104
00:07:19,954 --> 00:07:24,934
Colonel Darius Sepahvand could guess who had written the five conditions.

105
00:07:24,974 --> 00:07:31,192
No single person. They were the words that had crossed the command network for three weeks.

106
00:07:31,232 --> 00:07:34,355
We must be paid. This must never happen again.

107
00:07:34,395 --> 00:07:38,825
The strait is ours. Scattered anger had gathered into five lines.

108
00:07:38,865 --> 00:07:44,072
And once words become writing, the writing becomes history.

109
00:07:44,422 --> 00:07:47,311
The signals officer read out good news.

110
00:07:47,351 --> 00:07:49,639
Kuwait airport, fuel depot hit.

111
00:07:49,679 --> 00:07:52,418
A few men in the command post nodded.

112
00:07:52,458 --> 00:07:54,671
Darius should have nodded too.

113
00:07:54,711 --> 00:07:56,848
It was a professional reflex.

114
00:07:56,888 --> 00:08:00,829
The reflex rose to the back of his neck, and stopped.

115
00:08:00,869 --> 00:08:03,682
His daughter Narges's voice was there.

116
00:08:03,722 --> 00:08:07,588
Father, what did the people of Kuwait ever do to us?

117
00:08:07,628 --> 00:08:10,580
He had not answered her.

118
00:08:10,930 --> 00:08:17,872
In the summer of 1988, the day it accepted a ceasefire, this country called it a poisoned chalice.

119
00:08:17,912 --> 00:08:23,073
If these conditions were met and the war ended, how would Narges see him?

120
00:08:23,113 --> 00:08:28,416
As a father who won? Or as the man who nodded when Kuwait's airport burned?

121
00:08:28,456 --> 00:08:31,052
Today he did not nod. No one noticed.

122
00:08:31,092 --> 00:08:34,664
It was the only thing he could do.

123
00:08:35,014 --> 00:08:39,129
Seven-year-old Nariman came carrying a paper cup in both hands.

124
00:08:39,169 --> 00:08:43,679
Professor Azad Moradi had been in the hospital basement for six days.

125
00:08:43,719 --> 00:08:50,340
He had come for his blood pressure pills and found the children of doctors and nurses in the shelter.

126
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:53,242
Schools had been closed for twenty-six days.

127
00:08:53,282 --> 00:08:55,880
He told them stories from the Shahnameh.

128
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:59,180
The children called him Grandpa.

129
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:02,084
"Grandpa, drink this if you're cold."

130
00:09:02,124 --> 00:09:04,398
The cocoa was warm. It was sweet.

131
00:09:04,438 --> 00:09:06,432
Even in war, cocoa was sweet.

132
00:09:06,472 --> 00:09:13,865
The world could change, and sweet things stayed sweet; that was what it meant for a civilization to go on.

133
00:09:13,905 --> 00:09:17,090
"Today some men from Pakistan brought a paper.

134
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:23,960
But our country said no. Then why did they bring it, if we were going to say no?"

135
00:09:24,310 --> 00:09:26,327
"It's how grown-ups do it.

136
00:09:26,367 --> 00:09:30,837
First you say no once, and then the real talking starts."

137
00:09:30,877 --> 00:09:33,527
"That's weird." "Yes. It's weird."

138
00:09:33,567 --> 00:09:40,410
After a long while she asked, "Grandpa, when the war is over, will spring be prettier?"

139
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:42,467
Again he could not answer.

140
00:09:42,507 --> 00:09:45,473
Far away a siren rose and fell silent.

141
00:09:45,513 --> 00:09:50,540
The two of them drank their cocoa without a word.

142
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:55,529
Five lines arrived. Leila read them in the original Persian.

143
00:09:55,569 --> 00:09:58,414
An end to attacks and assassinations.

144
00:09:58,454 --> 00:10:00,832
Guarantees against another war.

145
00:10:00,872 --> 00:10:04,107
Reparations. An end to war on every front.

146
00:10:04,147 --> 00:10:06,291
Sovereignty over the strait.

147
00:10:06,331 --> 00:10:14,245
Behind each sentence stood an old wound: the coup of 1953, eight years of war, the years of sanctions.

148
00:10:14,285 --> 00:10:19,568
Not an enemy's history. Her aunt's country's history.

149
00:10:19,918 --> 00:10:22,299
She opened the resignation file.

150
00:10:22,339 --> 00:10:24,342
This time she pressed save.

151
00:10:24,382 --> 00:10:26,461
Then she deleted it at once.

152
00:10:26,501 --> 00:10:30,017
Not tonight. The five lines had to be analyzed.

153
00:10:30,057 --> 00:10:34,406
She had to find a way to keep the Islamabad channel alive.

154
00:10:34,446 --> 00:10:40,006
If she left, no one in this building would be left to read Fordow's pulse.

155
00:10:40,046 --> 00:10:43,844
Midnight passed. She began to type.

156
00:10:44,194 --> 00:10:49,158
Ending assassinations and guarantees against recurrence: real demands.

157
00:10:49,198 --> 00:10:54,590
Reparations: leverage. Sovereignty over the strait: the wording cannot bend,

158
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:58,807
but in practice it can be worked out through transit rules.

159
00:10:58,847 --> 00:11:00,952
And what was missing: nuclear.

160
00:11:00,992 --> 00:11:02,882
Not one word of enrichment.

161
00:11:02,922 --> 00:11:10,530
The documents that end wars usually come from the hands of those who wrote the documents that started them.

162
00:11:10,570 --> 00:11:11,720
— End of Episode 9. Continued in Episode 10. —

163
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:16,664
Epic Fury continues in Episode 10.
